For a fixed row \\(i\\) denote by \\(r_i\\) the number of tiles that intersect row \\(i\\).  \nIf the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) lies at column \\(1\\) or column \\(n\\), the covered part of the row is a single contiguous block, hence \\(r_i\\ge 1\\).  \nIf the uncovered square lies at an interior column \\(2\\le \\pi(i)\\le n-1\\), the covered part of the row splits into two disjoint blocks (to the left and to the right of the uncovered cell), so at least two different tiles are needed to cover them; therefore \\(r_i\\ge 2\\).\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nThen rows \\(1\\) and \\(n\\) have their uncovered squares on the border, while rows \\(2,\\dots ,n-1\\) have interior uncovered squares. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} r_i \\;\\ge\\; 1+1+2\\,(n-2)=2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nDefine analogously \\(c_j\\) as the number of tiles intersecting column \\(j\\).  \nThe same reasoning applied to the columns (which, under the diagonal permutation, also have border uncovered squares only in columns \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n} c_j \\;\\ge\\; 2n-2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery tile occupies at least one row and at least one column; therefore each tile is counted at most once in the sum \\(\\sum r_i\\) and at most once in the sum \\(\\sum c_j\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n2m \\;\\ge\\; \\sum_{i=1}^{n} r_i + \\sum_{j=1}^{n} c_j\n        \\;\\overset{(1),(2)}{\\ge}\\; (2n-2)+(2n-2)=4n-4 .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(2\\) gives the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\nm \\;\\ge\\; 2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nWe now exhibit a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, thereby showing that bound (3) is tight.\n\nTake again the diagonal permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Horizontal tiles.**  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_i