rd is a\nvalid tiling of size \\(n-1\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis the sub\u2011board needs at least\n\\(2(n-1)-2\\) tiles.  Adding the horizontal tile of the first row we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;1+\\bigl[2(n-1)-2\\bigr]=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling of an \\(n\\times n\\) board uses at least \\(2n-2\\)\ntiles.  The induction is complete.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction using exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nWe now exhibit a tiling that meets the lower bound.\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(r)=r\\) for all \\(r\\); i.e. the hole in row \\(r\\)\nis the diagonal square \\((r,r)\\).  (Any permutation would do, but the diagonal\nchoice makes the description simple.)\n\nDefine the following rectangles.\n\n*Horizontal rectangles.*  \nFor each row index \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,c)\\mid i\\le c\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe sub\u2011row of row \\(i\\) that starts at the diagonal square \\((i,i)\\) and\nextends to the right edge of the board.  Each \\(H_i\\) is a rectangle of size\n\\(1\\times (n-i+1)\\).\n\n*Vertical rectangles.*  \nFor each column index \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(r,j)\\mid j+1\\le r\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe sub\u2011column of column \\(j\\) that starts just below the diagonal square\n\\((j,j)\\) and extends to the bottom edge.  Each \\(V_j\\) is a rectangle of size\n\\((n-j)\\times 1\\).\n\nThere are \\((n-1)\\) horizontal rectangles and \\((n-1)\\) vertical rectangles,\nhence a total of \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n*Verification that the rectangles are disjoint.*  \n- Every cell of a horizontal rectangle \\(H_i\\) satisfies \\(r=i\\) and\n  \\(c\\ge i\\).  \n- Every cell of a vertical rectangle \\(V_j\\) satisfies \\(c=j\\) and\n  \\(r\\ge j+1\\).  \nThus a cell cannot belong simultaneously to an \\(H_i\\) and a \\(V_j\\), because\nthat would require \\(r=i=j\\) and simultaneously \\(r\\ge j+1\\), an impossibility