wer bound  \n\nAdding (3) and (4) we obtain the universal lower bound for any admissible\ntiling:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{#tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2n-2 } .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal\n\\((i,i)\\).\n\n* **Above the diagonal** (\\(i<j\\)).  \n  For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a rectangle covering rows\n  \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) and columns \\(k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\).  \n  Its dimensions are \\(k\\times (n-k)\\).  \n  These \\(n-1\\) rectangles exactly fill the whole region above the diagonal,\n  and none of them contains a diagonal square.\n\n* **Below the diagonal** (\\(i>j\\)).  \n  Symmetrically, for each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a rectangle covering rows\n  \\(k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\) and columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\).  \n  Its dimensions are \\((n-k)\\times k\\).  \n  Again we obtain \\(n-1\\) rectangles that fill the region below the diagonal.\n\nAll unit squares except the diagonal ones are covered, each row and each\ncolumn contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one), and the\ntotal number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Minimality  \n\nBecause the construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles and (5) shows that no\ntiling can use fewer, the number \\(2n-2\\) is minimal.\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the minimum number of rectangular tiles required is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
