re: n=2025 is a perfect square (45^2). Does the solution exploit that? Not needed; they use generic construction. Could there be a better solution using block decomposition? Possibly but not obvious. The lower bound seems tight; it's unlikely that perfect square structure yields better. So no suspicious optimality.\n\nThus Level 1.5 passes: no red flags.\n\nNow Level 2: Check reasoning validity.\n\nWe need to see if the solution uses valid mathematical principles. The solution uses combinatorial reasoning, lemma about rectangles, counting argument, explicit construction. All are valid methods. The lemma uses reasoning about rectangle containing two squares of A leads to diagonal square. That's a valid geometric argument. The lower bound uses counting and pigeonhole principle essentially. The construction uses explicit definition of tiles and disjointness argument. All valid.\n\nThus Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality.\n\nWe need to identify any justification gaps or critical errors. The solution is fairly rigorous. However, there might be minor gaps: For the lower bound, they claim \"A rectangle cannot serve simultaneously for covering a square of A and a square of B (the two sets lie on opposite sides of the diagonal)\". This is intuitive but they didn't prove it formally; but it's clear: any rectangle covering a square of A must be to the right of diagonal, any covering a square of B must be below diagonal; a rectangle covering both would have to include a diagonal square. That's essentially similar to Lemma 1. So it's fine.\n\nThe construction's disjointness argument: They argue if a square belongs to both a horizontal and vertical tile, we would have i=j and i<j contradictory. That's valid.\n\nCoverage argument: They claim any square (r,c) with r<c lies in H_r because c >= r+1. That's correct. Similarly for r>c. So coverage is fine.\n\nPotential minor justification gap: They didn't explicitly argue that the horizontal tiles H_i are disjoint fro