 arguments below are made for this configuration; any other permutation of uncovered squares can be transformed into it by the same row/column permutations, which do not change the number of tiles needed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\\qquad   \nB=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(A\\) consists of the squares immediately to the right of the diagonal, \\(B\\) those immediately below it.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nNo axis\u2011aligned rectangle that avoids all squares of \\(U\\) can contain two distinct squares of \\(A\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contains \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nLet the row interval of \\(R\\) be \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and the column interval be \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  \nBecause \\((i,i+1)\\in R\\) we have \\(r_1\\le i\\le r_2\\) and \\(c_1\\le i+1\\le c_2\\); similarly \\((j,j+1)\\in R\\) gives \\(r_1\\le j\\le r_2\\) and \\(c_1\\le j+1\\le c_2\\).  \nConsequently the rectangle contains all rows from \\(i\\) to \\(j\\) and all columns from \\(i+1\\) to \\(j+1\\).  \nIn particular the cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) (which lies on the diagonal) belongs to this row\u2011column product, so \\((i+1,i+1)\\in R\\).  \nThis contradicts the assumption that \\(R\\) avoids all diagonal squares. \u220e\n\nAn identical argument (by symmetry) shows that no rectangle avoiding \\(U\\) can contain two distinct squares of \\(B\\).\n\n---\n\nSince a rectangle can cover at most one element of \\(A\\), at least \\(|A|=n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover all squares of \\(A\\).  \nThe same reasoning applied to \\(B\\) yields another \\(n-1\\) necessary rectangles.  \nA rectangle cannot serve simultaneously for covering a square of \\(A\\) and a square of \\(B\\) (the two sets lie on opposite sides of the diagonal), therefore any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the **horizontal tile**\n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\); its sides lie on grid lines, and it does not contain the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the **vertical tile**\n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times 1\\); it avoids the uncovered square \\((j,j)\\).\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nEvery square of a horizontal tile satisfies row\u202f\\(=i\\) and column\u202f\\(>i\\); every square of a vertical tile satisfies column\u202f\\(=j\\) and row\u202f\\(>j\\).  \nIf a square belonged to both a horizontal and a vertical tile we would have simultaneously \\(i=j\\) (from equality of row and column) and \\(i<j\\) (from the \u201c\\(>\\)\u201d condition), an impossibility. Hence the \\(2n-2\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Coverage.*  \n- Any square \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r<c\\) (i.e. above the diagonal) lies in the horizontal tile \\(H_r\\) because its column \\(c\\) satisfies \\(c\\ge r+1\\).  \n- Any square \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r>c\\) (below the diagonal) lies in the vertical tile \\(V_c\\) because its row \\(r\\) satisfies \\(r\\ge c+1\\).  \n\nThus every non\u2011diagonal unit square is covered by exactly one tile, while the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered. Consequently each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered square, as required.\n\nThe construction uses exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nFrom the lower bound we have \\(\\text{tiles}\\ge 2n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction shows \\(\\text{tiles}=2n-2\\) is attainable.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is \\boxed{4048}."
